1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,460
A researcher tells you
that the number six no longer exists.

2
00:00:04,235 --> 00:00:07,615
Not that you shouldn't use it.
It has simply gone.

3
00:00:08,365 --> 00:00:10,285
Now count from one to ten.

4
00:00:10,845 --> 00:00:13,425
Some people skipped
the number six entirely.

5
00:00:14,125 --> 00:00:16,165
Then came a page of sums, full of sixes.

6
00:00:17,233 --> 00:00:20,533
This was real research,
by Frederick Evans and colleagues.

7
00:00:21,353 --> 00:00:23,903
Some of the volunteers were hypnotised.

8
00:00:24,253 --> 00:00:26,393
The others had been told to fake it.

9
00:00:26,954 --> 00:00:29,214
The fakers did the sensible thing.

10
00:00:29,754 --> 00:00:32,054
They turned the sixes
into sevens or zeroes.

11
00:00:33,175 --> 00:00:36,355
The hypnotised group
tended to do something much stranger.

12
00:00:37,095 --> 00:00:39,535
They worked as if the sixes weren't there.

13
00:00:40,099 --> 00:00:43,219
The fakers were solving a puzzle
about a missing number.

14
00:00:43,519 --> 00:00:46,379
For the others,
the number seemed to be missing.

15
00:00:47,045 --> 00:00:49,905
So for some people,
something real is going on.

16
00:00:50,365 --> 00:00:51,985
But how far does it go?

17
00:00:52,469 --> 00:00:54,889
Can someone plant a command in your head?

18
00:00:55,469 --> 00:00:57,529
Make you do something
you'd normally refuse?

19
00:00:58,269 --> 00:00:59,549
Change what you remember?

20
00:01:00,206 --> 00:01:02,406
All of that has been tested in a lab.

21
00:01:03,206 --> 00:01:05,046
And the trick that makes the answers
worth anything

22
00:01:05,276 --> 00:01:08,546
is the one you just saw:
test the fakers too.

23
00:01:10,126 --> 00:01:13,146
You've probably seen hypnosis already,
in films.

24
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:16,326
A doctor sends a sleepwalker out to kill.

25
00:01:16,926 --> 00:01:20,326
A soldier turns assassin
at the sight of a playing card.

26
00:01:20,935 --> 00:01:24,035
The hypnotist has a swinging
watch and a special power.

27
00:01:24,875 --> 00:01:28,795
The victim sinks into something like
sleep, obeys, remembers nothing,

28
00:01:29,025 --> 00:01:31,815
and stays under until someone
snaps their fingers.

29
00:01:32,532 --> 00:01:34,512
First, forget the swinging watch.

30
00:01:35,252 --> 00:01:36,852
Hypnosis isn't sleep.

31
00:01:37,392 --> 00:01:39,572
The person is awake and paying attention.

32
00:01:40,376 --> 00:01:42,416
It doesn't even take relaxing.

33
00:01:42,856 --> 00:01:45,916
Researchers have hypnotised people
with their eyes open,

34
00:01:46,146 --> 00:01:48,476
while they pedalled an exercise bike.

35
00:01:49,157 --> 00:01:52,337
A hypnotist narrows your attention,
then offers suggestions.

36
00:01:53,337 --> 00:01:55,017
Your arm is getting heavy.

37
00:01:55,497 --> 00:01:57,157
This water tastes sweet.

38
00:01:57,854 --> 00:02:00,674
When it works,
it doesn't feel like being overpowered.

39
00:02:01,514 --> 00:02:04,094
The arm just seems to get heavy by itself.

40
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:07,254
For many researchers,
that feeling is what makes it

41
00:02:07,484 --> 00:02:09,574
a suggestion rather than an instruction.

42
00:02:10,391 --> 00:02:11,871
And the special power?

43
00:02:12,471 --> 00:02:16,811
One of the standard tests in hypnosis
research comes with a recorded voice.

44
00:02:17,791 --> 00:02:22,711
It belongs to a radio announcer, with
no training in psychology or hypnosis.

45
00:02:23,441 --> 00:02:27,121
In the lab, at least, nearly all the
action is in the person being hypnotised.

46
00:02:28,801 --> 00:02:30,701
What about getting stuck in a trance?

47
00:02:31,561 --> 00:02:36,121
In a study published in 1971,
volunteers were hypnotised

48
00:02:36,351 --> 00:02:40,101
by a tape recording,
and a second group was told to fake it.

49
00:02:40,581 --> 00:02:43,261
Then the light went out
and the tape stopped.

50
00:02:44,021 --> 00:02:46,581
The researcher cursed
the fuse and rushed out.

51
00:02:47,228 --> 00:02:50,648
It was staged, and they were being watched
through a one-way screen.

52
00:02:51,288 --> 00:02:54,788
Five of the six fakers opened
their eyes within seconds.

53
00:02:55,488 --> 00:02:57,448
When they heard footsteps coming back,

54
00:02:57,528 --> 00:02:59,808
they shut them again
and carried on faking.

55
00:03:00,469 --> 00:03:04,149
For several minutes, most of the
hypnotised group didn't change at all.

56
00:03:04,769 --> 00:03:08,709
Then, slowly and with difficulty,
they brought themselves out of it.

57
00:03:09,369 --> 00:03:13,769
Those who came out opened their eyes
after about sixteen minutes, on average.

58
00:03:14,569 --> 00:03:16,209
Nobody snapped any fingers.

59
00:03:18,159 --> 00:03:21,919
On standard tests, about one
person in eight responds strongly.

60
00:03:21,999 --> 00:03:25,079
About as many barely respond at all.

61
00:03:25,159 --> 00:03:27,759
Everyone else is somewhere in between.

62
00:03:28,257 --> 00:03:30,157
And it doesn't mean you're gullible.

63
00:03:30,897 --> 00:03:34,057
It's linked more closely to absorption,
getting lost in a book

64
00:03:34,287 --> 00:03:37,457
or a piece of music,
than to how easily you're persuaded.

65
00:03:38,381 --> 00:03:40,041
Keep that top one in eight in mind,

66
00:03:40,271 --> 00:03:44,441
because most of the strangest
results in this video come from them.

67
00:03:46,831 --> 00:03:51,551
In 1939, a psychologist called Rowland
put a rattlesnake in a box

68
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:55,031
and asked hypnotised
volunteers to pick it up.

69
00:03:55,546 --> 00:03:59,686
A pane of invisible glass was in the way.
They couldn't see it.

70
00:04:00,166 --> 00:04:04,286
Three of the four reached for the snake,
one of them told it was a coil of rope.

71
00:04:04,890 --> 00:04:08,450
Then he invited forty-two ordinary people
to do the same.

72
00:04:08,530 --> 00:04:11,530
All but one wouldn't even go near the box.

73
00:04:12,305 --> 00:04:16,085
It looked like proof.
Hypnosis could switch off fear.

74
00:04:17,355 --> 00:04:20,895
In 1960, in Sydney,
Martin Orne and Frederick Evans

75
00:04:21,125 --> 00:04:23,175
ran it again, with one change.

76
00:04:24,001 --> 00:04:26,121
A venomous snake behind the glass.

77
00:04:26,381 --> 00:04:30,141
Then a beaker of real nitric acid,
dissolving a coin.

78
00:04:30,801 --> 00:04:33,601
Take the coin out with your bare hand,
they said.

79
00:04:34,170 --> 00:04:37,250
After that, throw the acid
in the assistant's face.

80
00:04:38,010 --> 00:04:41,390
By then, it had been secretly swapped
for a harmless liquid.

81
00:04:42,055 --> 00:04:47,475
Five of the six hypnotised volunteers did
all of it, just like in the older studies.

82
00:04:48,167 --> 00:04:49,447
Here's the change.

83
00:04:50,147 --> 00:04:54,547
They also tested six people who couldn't
be hypnotised, and told them to fake it.

84
00:04:55,113 --> 00:04:56,953
All six went for it too.

85
00:04:57,383 --> 00:05:01,483
Then six more, wide awake,
with no hypnosis and nothing to fake.

86
00:05:01,903 --> 00:05:04,643
Five of them still did both acid tasks.

87
00:05:05,460 --> 00:05:08,160
Afterwards,
they nearly all gave the same reason.

88
00:05:08,860 --> 00:05:12,220
This was an experiment,
run by responsible scientists.

89
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,320
So it had to be safe.

90
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:19,700
Some assumed the snake had been defanged,
or that the acid wasn't really acid.

91
00:05:20,303 --> 00:05:22,323
Orne and Evans also
asked a few colleagues,

92
00:05:22,553 --> 00:05:24,943
informally, to try the same tasks.

93
00:05:25,683 --> 00:05:27,503
They refused every time.

94
00:05:28,081 --> 00:05:30,921
So reaching into acid
didn't take hypnosis.

95
00:05:31,701 --> 00:05:33,241
It only took the setting.

96
00:05:33,939 --> 00:05:36,039
That doesn't prove
hypnosis can't be misused.

97
00:05:36,269 --> 00:05:39,539
Orne and Evans were clear about that.

98
00:05:40,079 --> 00:05:42,079
It means this test can't
answer the question,

99
00:05:42,309 --> 00:05:46,079
because the laboratory setting
can make people assume they're safe.

100
00:05:48,739 --> 00:05:51,499
It also explains a lot about stage shows.

101
00:05:52,239 --> 00:05:54,279
The volunteers chose to be there.

102
00:05:54,679 --> 00:05:56,279
The audience is watching.

103
00:05:56,639 --> 00:05:58,939
Everyone knows what's meant to happen.

104
00:05:59,486 --> 00:06:01,646
Some of them may genuinely feel it.

105
00:06:01,746 --> 00:06:05,766
But someone clucking like a chicken
on stage tells you very little

106
00:06:05,996 --> 00:06:08,306
about what a hypnotist
could do to a stranger.

107
00:06:09,699 --> 00:06:11,579
So is it all just the situation?

108
00:06:11,739 --> 00:06:12,939
Not quite.

109
00:06:13,621 --> 00:06:16,921
In a study published in 1968,
Orne's team gave

110
00:06:17,151 --> 00:06:20,281
seventeen highly responsive volunteers
a command.

111
00:06:21,301 --> 00:06:24,721
For the next two days,
whenever you hear the word experiment,

112
00:06:24,881 --> 00:06:27,301
your hand will rise
and touch your forehead.

113
00:06:27,834 --> 00:06:29,794
Then they told them to forget it.

114
00:06:30,354 --> 00:06:32,694
Fourteen fakers got the same instruction.

115
00:06:33,434 --> 00:06:35,254
In the lab, they all responded.

116
00:06:36,147 --> 00:06:38,287
The real test was somewhere else.

117
00:06:38,767 --> 00:06:41,207
It was the secretary in the waiting room.

118
00:06:41,771 --> 00:06:44,911
As they left, she mentioned
the next part of the experiment.

119
00:06:45,711 --> 00:06:49,591
The next day, she asked:
are you here for Dr Sheehan's experiment?

120
00:06:50,381 --> 00:06:53,321
Not one of the fakers
responded on both days.

121
00:06:54,018 --> 00:06:56,298
Five of the hypnotised group did.

122
00:06:56,878 --> 00:06:59,598
A hand rising to the forehead,
for a secretary.

123
00:07:00,809 --> 00:07:03,389
So a command really
can outlast the session,

124
00:07:03,469 --> 00:07:05,449
with the hypnotist out of the room.

125
00:07:06,068 --> 00:07:07,508
Now the small print.

126
00:07:08,128 --> 00:07:12,808
That's five out of seventeen, all picked
because they responded exceptionally well.

127
00:07:13,198 --> 00:07:17,678
And near the end, when it seemed obvious
the researcher was about to cancel it,

128
00:07:17,758 --> 00:07:20,858
only two of the hypnotised
group still responded.

129
00:07:21,614 --> 00:07:26,854
So it isn't software. It isn't guaranteed,
and the situation can switch it off.

130
00:07:29,574 --> 00:07:32,474
But suggestion can reach further in
than you'd expect.

131
00:07:33,354 --> 00:07:34,512
Try this.

132
00:07:34,592 --> 00:07:38,232
Say the colour of each word.
Not the word. The colour.

133
00:07:44,285 --> 00:07:47,125
You felt that.
Reading happens automatically.

134
00:07:47,705 --> 00:07:51,025
Even when you're trying to ignore
the word, it gets in the way.

135
00:07:51,637 --> 00:07:55,857
In 2002, Amir Raz and his colleagues
gave people a suggestion

136
00:07:56,087 --> 00:08:00,537
to stop reading those words,
and pay attention only to the ink.

137
00:08:01,183 --> 00:08:05,043
In sixteen highly suggestible people,
the delay disappeared.

138
00:08:05,843 --> 00:08:08,783
In sixteen less suggestible people,
it didn't.

139
00:08:09,239 --> 00:08:11,919
A later scan of a smaller group
found less activity

140
00:08:12,149 --> 00:08:16,159
in the area linked to conflict,
and in the visual areas too.

141
00:08:16,759 --> 00:08:20,239
A later study reduced that delay
with the suggestion alone,

142
00:08:20,519 --> 00:08:23,759
and no hypnosis,
in highly suggestible people.

143
00:08:24,492 --> 00:08:26,612
Nobody took those people over.

144
00:08:27,032 --> 00:08:29,212
They were doing something
to their own reading.

145
00:08:29,852 --> 00:08:31,792
Exactly how is still debated.

146
00:08:34,402 --> 00:08:37,502
The most unsettling results, though,
are about memory.

147
00:08:38,253 --> 00:08:40,973
In films, a hypnotist erases a memory.

148
00:08:41,593 --> 00:08:44,093
What's been shown
in the lab is far weaker.

149
00:08:44,753 --> 00:08:48,173
People can be told to forget
what happened during the session itself.

150
00:08:48,753 --> 00:08:52,773
It's temporary.
Give the agreed cue, and it comes back.

151
00:08:53,418 --> 00:08:56,178
The opposite idea had a far better run:

152
00:08:56,378 --> 00:08:58,918
that hypnosis can recover
what you've forgotten.

153
00:09:00,101 --> 00:09:05,541
In 1976, in Chowchilla, California,
three men hijacked a school bus.

154
00:09:06,161 --> 00:09:08,301
They locked the driver
and twenty-six children

155
00:09:08,531 --> 00:09:12,561
in a truck trailer buried in a quarry.
All of them escaped.

156
00:09:13,342 --> 00:09:16,442
The driver, Ed Ray,
had seen the kidnappers' vans.

157
00:09:17,242 --> 00:09:21,102
Under hypnosis, he came up with their
number plates, all but one digit.

158
00:09:21,845 --> 00:09:24,225
It helped lead the police
to the kidnappers.

159
00:09:24,971 --> 00:09:28,671
Police in America were already
training officers in hypnosis.

160
00:09:29,391 --> 00:09:31,771
It looked as if the mind
keeps a recording,

161
00:09:31,851 --> 00:09:34,031
and you just need a way to play it back.

162
00:09:35,871 --> 00:09:41,791
In 1983, two researchers hypnotised
twenty-seven highly hypnotisable people.

163
00:09:42,371 --> 00:09:47,351
They suggested that, one night the week
before, loud noises had woken them up.

164
00:09:47,894 --> 00:09:49,094
That was invented.

165
00:09:49,704 --> 00:09:53,224
Afterwards, thirteen of them stated
that it had really happened.

166
00:09:53,575 --> 00:09:55,775
Nearly half, from one suggestion.

167
00:09:57,215 --> 00:10:02,275
In 1985, the American Medical Association
reviewed the evidence.

168
00:10:03,095 --> 00:10:07,155
Memories recovered under hypnosis
weren't more accurate, it concluded.

169
00:10:07,995 --> 00:10:10,795
They appeared less reliable
than ordinary recall.

170
00:10:11,565 --> 00:10:13,505
Ed Ray's plates could be checked.

171
00:10:14,165 --> 00:10:15,745
That's what made them useful.

172
00:10:16,525 --> 00:10:18,645
It doesn't show that hypnosis
improves memory,

173
00:10:18,875 --> 00:10:21,565
and most memories can't be checked at all.

174
00:10:22,097 --> 00:10:23,877
Memory isn't a recording.

175
00:10:24,637 --> 00:10:27,137
Each time you remember something,
you rebuild it,

176
00:10:27,217 --> 00:10:29,437
and a suggestion can get into the rebuild.

177
00:10:32,337 --> 00:10:34,517
So is hypnosis any use at all?

178
00:10:35,017 --> 00:10:37,057
Here, the answer is a clear yes.

179
00:10:37,547 --> 00:10:42,007
A 2019 review pooled eighty-five trials
of hypnosis against pain,

180
00:10:42,087 --> 00:10:45,067
all with pain induced
in healthy volunteers.

181
00:10:45,942 --> 00:10:48,022
With direct suggestions against pain,

182
00:10:48,102 --> 00:10:51,682
highly suggestible people
reported forty-two percent less.

183
00:10:52,522 --> 00:10:53,982
Medium, twenty-nine percent.

184
00:10:54,062 --> 00:10:56,002
Low, seventeen.

185
00:10:56,751 --> 00:11:01,131
In New York, two hundred patients
having breast surgery were randomly split.

186
00:11:01,851 --> 00:11:04,491
Some got fifteen minutes
of hypnosis beforehand.

187
00:11:05,451 --> 00:11:07,651
The rest got someone who just listened.

188
00:11:08,328 --> 00:11:10,328
The hypnosis group
needed less of two drugs,

189
00:11:10,558 --> 00:11:13,388
and reported about half as much pain.

190
00:11:14,148 --> 00:11:16,345
On average,
each patient cost seven hundred

191
00:11:16,575 --> 00:11:21,228
and seventy-two dollars less,
mainly because the surgery was shorter.

192
00:11:21,951 --> 00:11:26,291
The hypnosis came on top of normal
anaesthesia, not instead of it.

193
00:11:26,791 --> 00:11:29,471
Those patients knew
which group they were in,

194
00:11:29,551 --> 00:11:31,931
and it's no replacement for medical care.

195
00:11:32,567 --> 00:11:34,527
But notice what's happening here.

196
00:11:35,027 --> 00:11:36,747
Nobody is being controlled.

197
00:11:37,307 --> 00:11:39,967
Somebody agreed to it,
and their attention moved.

198
00:11:42,257 --> 00:11:44,417
So can someone program your mind?

199
00:11:44,777 --> 00:11:46,497
Not the way films show it.

200
00:11:46,817 --> 00:11:48,417
There's no remote control.

201
00:11:49,205 --> 00:11:50,825
But think about those volunteers.

202
00:11:51,425 --> 00:11:53,185
A hand rising at reception.

203
00:11:53,865 --> 00:11:56,025
A night of noises that never happened.

204
00:11:56,305 --> 00:11:58,945
A page of sums with no sixes.

205
00:11:59,545 --> 00:12:02,845
From the inside, a suggestion
doesn't feel like a suggestion.

206
00:12:03,525 --> 00:12:04,865
It feels like you.

207
00:12:08,355 --> 00:12:10,195
So here's a question for you.

208
00:12:10,715 --> 00:12:14,035
What's a memory you'd swear by,
and how would you actually check it?

209
00:12:14,375 --> 00:12:15,685
Let me know in the comments.
